! Four 3D elements, one of each family -- tetrahedron, PYRAMID, prism,
! hexahedron -- from ElementUnitTest's unitelems mesh, assembled equal order.
! A smoke test, not a convergence test: four elements sharing one boundary do not
! pose a physical problem, and the norm below only fingerprints the assembly.
! What it checks is that every 3D family this solver can meet actually assembles.
!
! That is worth a test because two defects lived here undetected, and this mesh
! could not be run by IncompressibleNSVec at all before they were dealt with:
!
!   - the PYRAMID cannot carry a bubble under the serendipity scheme, which is
!     the default. ElementInfoVec refuses it outright: "p-Pyramid not available
!     for serendipity scheme". So the MINI element cannot be used on any mesh
!     containing a pyramid unless the sif also sets
!     "Serendipity P Elements = False" -- a mixed mesh with a hex-to-tet
!     transition layer will meet this.
!
!   - and with the full basis selected, the PRISM used to segfault, _Init0 asking
!     for b:4 where that basis ladder runs 0, 2, 9, 24. Fixed separately.
!
! The equal-order pair sidesteps the first of those entirely: "n:1" is not a
! p-element, so the serendipity pyramid restriction does not apply to it. It runs
! here on the default settings, which the MINI element cannot. That is a
! capability difference between the two pairs, not a tuning one, and it is the
! reason this test exists in the stabilised form only.
!
! KNOWN, NOT FIXED: the MINI variant of this case fails above two OpenMP threads,
! and does so whatever integration rule it is given. Four elements of four
! different families assembled concurrently is the condition; the equal-order
! variant here is unaffected at any thread count. Not investigated, and not
! caused by the stabilisation -- it is in the p-element assembly path.
Header
  Mesh DB "." "unitelems"
  Include Path ""
  Results Directory ""
End

Simulation
  Max Output Level = 5
  Coordinate System = Cartesian 3D
  Simulation Type = Steady
  Steady State Max Iterations = 1
  Output Intervals = 0

! Required, not incidental: see the note above on the pyramid.
! Deliberately left at the default. The equal-order element is not a p-element,
! so the serendipity pyramid restriction does not apply to it -- this variant
! runs on a pyramid mesh that the MINI one above cannot without the line removed
! here. That is a capability difference, not a tuning one.
End

Constants
End

Body 1
  Equation = 1
  Material = 1
End

Equation 1
  Active Solvers(1) = 1
End

Solver 1
  Equation = "NS-Vec"
  Procedure = "IncompressibleNSVec" "IncompressibleNSSolver"
  Stokes Flow = Logical True
  Pressure Stabilization = Logical True
  Element = "n:1"

  Solver Timing = Logical True
  Bulk Assembly Timing = Logical True
  Linear System Timing = Logical True

  Linear System Solver = Direct
  Linear System Direct Method = umfpack

  Nonlinear System Max Iterations = 1
  Steady State Convergence Tolerance = 1.0e-5
End

Material 1
  Density = 1
  Viscosity = 1.0
End

Boundary Condition 1
  Target Boundaries(1) = 1
  Velocity 1 = 0.1
  Velocity 2 = 0
  Velocity 3 = 0
End

! The two variants give the SAME norm, and that is expected rather than a sign
! they are not really different: the exact solution here is uniform flow, which
! both the bubble-augmented and the equal-order basis represent exactly. They do
! differ -- 64 integration points against 8 -- but on this problem neither has
! anything to approximate. Treat the number as a fingerprint of the assembly, not
! as a comparison between the pairs.
Solver 1 :: Reference Norm = 4.17028830E-02
